rial  enzyme glutamines  glutamine is indeed supplied by the glia  so glia supply glutamine which  gets into the presynaptic terminal with  specific transporters and that gets  converted to glutamate with the help of  glutamines enzyme  once glutamate is synthesized it gets  packaged into synaptic vesicles  with the help of v-glute  transporters or vesicular glutamate  transporters  and ultimately these synaptic vesicles  are released into the post-synapse  now once it's present in the synaptic  cleft glutamate doesn't stay for long  time  it gets reuptaken by the glia and neuron  with the help of excitatory amino acid  transporters  now inside the glia glutamate gets  converted to glutamine again  with the help of enzyme glutamine  synthetase  and that glutamine is again supplied  back to the presynaptic terminal for  further glutamate biosynthesis  alternatively a small proportion of  glutamate is directly uptaken into the  synaptic terminal as well  now this complicated stepwise procedure  is known as the glutamate cycle  and that's what keeps our excitatory  synapses going  the glutamate synapse is one of the  excitatory synapses there are many other  but in this video let's focus on  glutamate and how they work  at a synaptic level  in order to understand that we need to  understand glutamate receptor and their  subtypes  glutamate receptors are broadly divided  into two categories ionotropic glutamate  receptors and metabotropic glutamate  receptors  under ionotropic glutamate receptors we  can see ampa receptors kinetic receptors  and nmd receptors all these nomenclature  are done based on the agonist  of these ion channels  now there are metabotropic receptors  which are specific uh g protein coupled  receptors and they work using several  second messengers and intermediate  players  and these are referred as  m glu r  if you wanted details about metabotropic  and ionotropic receptor and how they  work you can quickly get it in the eye  button  now  in short ionotropic receptors that are  ligand-gated ion channel are very fast  in terms of their action so it's just  ligand binding to the channel which  leads to channel opening and influx of  the ion so it would rapidly depolarize  or change the potential of a neuron  whereas metabotropic receptors  are slow in nature because they have to  generate intermediate players  intermediate players would then  coordinate with each other to bring out  the cellular response  the first type of  ionotropic glutamate receptor that we  are going to talk about is ampa  receptors  ampa receptors are found in the post  synapse in the glutamatergic neurons  when the presynaptic action potential  reach the nerve terminal it releases  glutamate into the cleft and let's see  what happens in the post synapse in the  post synapse we would have ampa  receptors which binds to glutamate and  allows the influx of cations inside the  neuron  so cations flow inside and it leads to a  positive potential in the membrane so  the membrane becomes more positive which  leads to a post synaptic potential or  postsynaptic current  in order to understand the kinetics of  these kind of receptors we can look at  their physiological properties by  electrophysiological recordings in a  patch clamp settings in a whole cell  mode if we  hold it in a voltage clamp method we can  stabilize the voltage at a particular  defined voltage and we can look at the  current like here  so if we hold amber receptors as at  minus 70 millivolts we would see a  inward current  and you see the current  differs with the voltage  and you can see as the voltage increases  towards more positive side the direction  of the current is reversed  another way of understanding this is to  plot a iv plot or  cur or current versus voltage plot or gv  plot or conductance versus voltage plot  anyway for ample receptors this plot  would look like  this  and the gv plot would look like a flat  line  by looking at the plot we can understand  there