nd the positive potential inside would  repel the magnesium ion away from the  nmd receptors channel which would allow  the influx of cations through the nmd  receptor to the post synapse and  these cations are not only sodium or  potassium calcium  also get in through the through this  nmda receptor  let's talk about the  electrophysiological properties of the  nmda receptor  again we are going to use a voltage  clamp recording so at negative potential  you can see the line is pretty much flat  so at minus 70 millivolt there is almost  no current minus 50 there is a minuscule  current  if we go closer like minus 10 which  which means the  membrane potential is bit more positive  compared to the resting membrane  potential which is minus 65 millivolt we  see some amount of inward current  and then at positive potential we do see  a current whose amplitude is much more  than the earlier ones  in any way we can plot a iv plot that we  have seen before for the nmda receptor  as well  and iv plot shows that it is  kind of voltage dependent  and now at  a potential which is  low  there is no current  a potential which is more than minus 50  and in between minus 50 and 0 there is  some amount of minuscule inward current  and the outward current is at  positive voltages  looking at the gv plot we can understand  that there is a voltage dependence in  terms of the conductance of the nmda  receptor  so almost there is no conductance at  negative voltage  at the positive voltage again there is  no uh correlation between voltage  increase or conductance but there is  some amount of conductance at positive  voltage  now let us try to understand this iv  plot  using this analogy so in this part of  the iv plot  the magnesium block is still there  that's why the cations cannot get in  whereas this part which is highlighted  in the curve you can see at this point  of time the magnesium block is kind of  repelled  and that is why there is influx of  cation through the nmda receptor  now as per summary  we looked at the nmd receptors reversion  potential from the graph we can say it  is zero millivolt  it conducts sodium potassium calcium and  single channel conductance for nmd  receptor is 50 picosecond so it give  rise to sustained currents compared to  ampa or kinate ampa and kyanet shows  very fast opening and closing kinetics  whereas nmda shows  fast opening but sustained or delayed  closing kinetics  now nmd receptors are known as  coincidence detector that means they  detect the coincidence between pre and  the postsynaptic activity  it detects the synchrony between pre and  post synapse let me tell you how  nmda receptor blockade has been shown  to block potentiation  so what is potentiation so this is how a  normal synapse would look like here you  can record from the  post synapse and stimulate the  pre-synapse so obviously as expected you  would see there is a  postsynaptic potential you can see like  this  now you do a hundred hertz tetanic  stimulation  and then record from the same synapse  now again if you give the same amount of  current as before  instead of getting same amplitude of the  response now one get a augmented  response or a boosted response  this is known as synaptic potentiation  if this potentiation lasts for long this  is known as long-term potentiation  another way is to plot  the slope of these post-synaptic  potentials  and compare it over time  so just after this tetanic stimulation  you can see the slope has increased and  it stays increased for long time  in simple words this is known as  long-term potentiation  fun fact is if we block nmda receptor  from an by a pharmacological agent one  can see  even after 100 hertz stimulation there  is no  potentiation of the synapses that means  nmd receptor is really important for  regulating synaptic strength  nmda receptor blockage lead to  behavioral defects in mouse  there is a there is a particular  behavioral task known as maurice water  maze where the mouse 