 is inward direction current at  negative voltage where there is outward  direction current at positive voltage  and the reversal potential is zero  now looking at the gv plot we can  understand by changing voltage there is  no change in the conductance its kind of  like a flat line so ampa receptors are  kind of independent to voltage and that  is expected because an ampa receptors  the conductance of the ampa receptor  does not depend on voltage because they  are  ligand-gated io channels they are not  voltage-gated ion channels right that's  why their conductance is voltage  independent  anyway  let's talk about the kinetic receptors  just like ampa kinet would be a  ligand-gated ion channel they would  conduct sodium and potassium  now these single channel current is  slightly  more than ampa receptors the single  channel current is  single channel conductance is actually  more than 20 picosecond  whereas for amp it is 10 to 15  picosecond  rise and decay time  for the postsynaptic potential generated  by kinetic receptors are slightly slower  compared to ampa but not as slow as the  nmd receptors that we are going to talk  about next  now nmda receptors are iron ligand-gated  ion channels but slightly different than  the  other ones so in the postsynaptic  terminal there is amp receptor which  lead to  cation influx upon binding to glutamate  and this cation influx  is uh making the membrane more positive  but the nmd receptor works in a  different principle  so when there is a cationic influx  through the ampa receptor at the same  potential lmd receptor might not be  active  because nmd receptor has a magnesium  block so imagine  the initial stage where the  inside of the membrane is still somewhat  negative at this point magnesium block  is there in the nmda channel's pore  that's why none of the cations can go  through the nmda receptor but after  conduct conductance of ampa receptor for  a long time what would happen is the  inside would become positive  and the positive potential inside would  repel the magnesium ion away from the  nmd receptors channel which would allow  the influx of cations through the nmd  receptor to the post synapse and  these cations are not only sodium or  potassium calcium  also get in through the through this  nmda receptor  let's talk about the  electrophysiological properties of the  nmda receptor  again we are going to use a voltage  clamp recording so at negative potential  you can see the line is pretty much flat  so at minus 70 millivolt there is almost  no current minus 50 there is a minuscule  current  if we go closer like minus 10 which  which means the  membrane potential is bit more positive  compared to the resting membrane  potential which is minus 65 millivolt we  see some amount of inward current  and then at positive potential we do see  a current whose amplitude is much more  than the earlier ones  in any way we can plot a iv plot that we  have seen before for the nmda receptor  as well  and iv plot shows that it is  kind of voltage dependent  and now at  a potential which is  low  there is no current  a potential which is more than minus 50  and in between minus 50 and 0 there is  some amount of minuscule inward current  and the outward current is at  positive voltages  looking at the gv plot we can understand  that there is a voltage dependence in  terms of the conductance of the nmda  receptor  so almost there is no conductance at  negative voltage  at the positive voltage again there is  no uh correlation between voltage  increase or conductance but there is  some amount of conductance at positive  voltage  now let us try to understand this iv  plot  using this analogy so in this part of  the iv plot  the magnesium block is still there  that's why the cations cannot get in  whereas this part which is highlighted  in the curve you can see at this point  of time the magnesium block is kind of  repelled  and that is why there is influx of  cation through the nmda receptor  now as per summary  we looked at the nmd receptors reversion  potential from the graph we can say it  is zero millivolt  it conducts sodium potassium calcium and  single channel conductance for nmd  receptor is 50 picosecond so it give  rise to sustained currents compared to  ampa or kinate ampa and kyanet shows  very fast opening and closing kinetics  whereas nmda shows  fast opening but sustained or delayed  closing kinetics  now nmd receptors are known as  coincidence detector that means they  detect the coincidence between pre and  the postsynaptic activity  it detects the synchrony between pre and  post synapse let me tell you how  nmda receptor blockade has been shown  to block potentiation  so what is potentiation so this is how a  normal synapse would look like here you  can record from the  post synapse and stimulate the  pre-synapse so obviously as expected you  would see there is a  postsynaptic potential you can see like  this  now you do a hundred hertz tetanic  stimulation  and then record from the same synapse  now again if you give the same amount of  current as before  instead of getting same amplitude of the  response now one get a augmented  response or a boosted response  this is known as synaptic potentiation  if this potentiation lasts for long this  is known as long-term potentiation  another way is to plot  the slope of these post-synaptic  potentials  and compare it over time  so just after this tetanic stimulation  you can see the slope has increased and  it stays increased for long time  in simple words this is known as  long-term potentiation  fun fact is if we block nmda receptor  from an by a pharmacological agent one  can see  even after 100 hertz stimulation there  is no  potentiation of the synapses that means  nmd receptor is really important for  regulating synaptic strength  nmda receptor blockage lead to  behavioral defects in mouse  there is a there is a particular  behavioral task known as maurice water  maze where the mouse has to find a  hidden platform in the tub and they  locate this platform with some kind of  landmarks so these are visual landmarks  with some training mouse can do this  task very nicely as seen in the control  so they directly go to the quadrant  where there is a visual landmark and  where there is a hidden platform but  when nmda receptor is blocked this  particular task is totally messed up by  the mouse so they cannot do it anymore  that means nmda receptors are associated  with memory formation  nmda receptors are ionotopic glutamate  receptors  and we looked at  that these receptors are found in  glutamatergic synapses they are  ligand-gated ion channels they regulate  synaptic strength and they are involved  in memory formation  now let's talk about  the uh  the metabotropic glutamate receptors  sorry it's written as muscarinic  acetylcholine receptors but imagine  these are metabotropic glutamate  receptors  so metabotropic glutamate receptors bind  with glutamate  they lead to  several  cellular events in this case they lead  to a gq coupled signaling which lead to  the activation of phospholipase c  that indeed activates second messengers  like  diacylglycerol and ip3  all these second messengers can lead to  cascade of events interesting cascade of  intracellular events  so group 1 mlrs  such as mglu r1 mgr5  use this kind of operation paradigm  whereas  gi coupled receptors are used  by several other classes of imbuers they  inhibit adenylate cycles adrenaline  cycles is an enzyme that generates  cyclic amp from atp  and  this particular  activity is blocked by gi coupled signal  and that kind of in turn  regulate how much of ionic influx would  be there through a cyclic nucleotide  gated ion channel and this kind of  activity is done by group 2 m gluers  such as imbuer 2 and 3 just to name a  few  so as per summary we have looked at  biosynthesis and packaging of glutamate  glutamate into the synaptic vesicle  we looked at how glutamate this  glutamate works and how glutamate  receptors work in the post synapse we  looked at the electrophysiological  properties of the glutamate receptors  we looked at how they regulate synaptic  strength  and we compare their  electrophysiological properties in terms  of iv and gv plot  so  and lastly we looked at some of these  receptors such as nmda receptors are  involved in memory formation and  synaptic plasticity if you want detailed  explanation of long-term potentiation  synaptic plasticity all of these are  linked in the i button  get notes and flash cards  in my facebook page you can also follow  us on instagram to download these  amazing flash cards which would help in  your preparation all the links are in  the description you can support my  channel using patreon bmupi app or you  can directly support using the super  thanks option which is present in the  bottom right corner of any video you can  contribute using paytm paypal or upi or  even internet banking  so  show me your support and show some love  don't forget to like share and subscribe  if you wish to connect you can get all  my social media handles in the  description you can also follow the nerd  medic channel for exclusive medical  content  as usual don't forget to like share and  subscribe


